Executive Summary

The QUENNE Hybrid Intelligent Star Ship (Q-HISS) proposes a next-generation deep-space platform built upon stacked intelligence architecture, integrating photonic processing, neuromorphic cognition, quantum-assisted optimization, and human-governed autonomy.

Future deep-space operations demand spacecraft capable of:

• Multi-year autonomous operation
• Navigation beyond Earth infrastructure
• Self-diagnosis and adaptive resilience
• Intelligent energy and resource management
• Human-AI cooperative mission control

Q-HISS addresses these requirements by embedding intelligence as a core spacecraft infrastructure layer, enabling machine-speed perception while preserving human authority.

The architecture extends QUENNE intelligence systems from atmospheric and orbital platforms into deep-space infrastructure.

⸻

1. Introduction

Traditional spacecraft architectures remain:

• Earth-dependent
• Limited in onboard decision autonomy
• Vulnerable to communication delays
• Resource inefficient in long missions

Deep-space missions introduce:

• Communication delays of minutes to hours
• Radiation exposure risks
• Long-duration hardware degradation
• Energy and thermal constraints
• Unknown mission conditions

Q-HISS introduces cognitive spacecraft architecture, where intelligence becomes integral to vehicle operation rather than auxiliary.

